{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2415","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2415","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2415\",\"parent_revision\":\"dda58fea33f5c15da2255ad9091ad746badf168096ddb62e4e99c83af3708762\",\"root_revision\":\"dda58fea33f5c15da2255ad9091ad746badf168096ddb62e4e99c83af3708762\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nWhen the CLAUDE.md directive \\\"if you see a problem that can be fixed now, just do it\\\" fires, there is no mechanism to:\\n\\n1. Gate on domain skill loading before acting \u2014 without loaded skills, agents treat plugin markdown files as static documentation rather than as functional behavioral engineering that changes workload outcomes.\\n2. Assess whether problem complexity qualifies for the quick path without explicit user approval \u2014 the decision to bypass full pipeline review is made silently.\\n3. Route the implementation through `--quick` so that hooks and MCP tool events process the issue state through the backend correctly \u2014 no `in-progress` label is set, no SAM plan is created, no audit trail exists.\\n\\nThe \\\"fix it now\\\" directive and the `--quick` workflow path (`/dh:work-backlog-item --quick`) are two independent execution paths with no coupling. Proactive fixes bypass the `dh` status machine entirely.\\n\\nObserved: session 2026-05-22 \u2014 backlog item #2371 was groomed and then directly implemented without invoking `--quick`, resulting in a missing `in-progress` transition and no SAM plan record. The fix was correctly scoped (one test file), but the workflow state was not tracked.\\n\\nThe issue also relates to how product improvement is demonstrated and measured against mission objectives when shortcuts are taken: without skill loading and without routing through the defined process, the agent cannot assess whether the change aligns with the system's design principles or meets the acceptance criteria defined in the plugin's behavioral contracts.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: The --quick path exists at references/workflows/quick/start.md\\nevidence: File present and readable at expected path\\nsource: plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/quick/start.md\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: The --quick path creates a SAM plan and sets in-progress tracking\\nevidence: quick/start.md lines 20\u201332 \u2014 calls backlog_update with plan parameter, sets in-progress label\\nsource: plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/quick/start.md:20-32\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: CLAUDE.md contains proactive-fix directive in Autonomous Action Boundary section\\nevidence: Table at lines 137\u2013146 contains: Fix errors discovered during current task \u2014 with no gate, no skill-loading requirement, no --quick coupling\\nsource: .claude/CLAUDE.md:137-146\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: CLAUDE.md does NOT reference --quick in the Autonomous Action Boundary section\\nevidence: grep for '--quick' in lines 137\u2013166 returned zero matches\\nsource: .claude/CLAUDE.md:137-166\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: Plugin SKILL.md files contain mission objectives and behavioral contracts\\nevidence: SKILL.md contains mission statement, behavioral contracts, and process documentation sections\\nsource: plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: Backlog item #2371 was implemented without invoking --quick, leaving workflow state untracked\\nevidence: #2371 closed via PR #2414; git log confirms direct implementation without --quick; no in-progress label was set, no SAM plan created\\nsource: GitHub issue #2371, PR #2414, commit a800496\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: feat: Proactive fix directive is decoupled from --quick path: no skill loading gate, no complexity check, no backend state tracking\\nDate: 2026-05-22\\nGoal: Couple the proactive-fix directive in CLAUDE.md and holistic-linting files to the --quick path, adding skill-loading gate, complexity check, and backend state tracking.\\n\\nDecision: APPROVED (amended 2026-05-22 after user resolved single MISSING condition)\\n\\n**Conditions (12/12 AVAILABLE):**\\n- AVAILABLE: CLAUDE.md lines 137\u2013146 (Autonomous Action Boundary) \u2014 current text confirmed on disk\\n- AVAILABLE: CLAUDE.md lines 262\u2013288 (Pre-Existing Issue Accountability + Request Progression) \u2014 current text confirmed on disk\\n- AVAILABLE: quick/start.md \u2014 full workflow confirmed; invocation form is flags.quick = true (flag, not subcommand)\\n- AVAILABLE: All five target file paths confirmed on disk\\n- AVAILABLE: Complexity eligibility criteria \u2014 deterministic from quick/start.md + feasibility-gate.md Criterion 4 (single-file, clear root cause, no design decision, no new skill required)\\n- AVAILABLE: plugins/holistic-linting/skills/holistic-linting/references/pre-existing-issues-protocol.md \u2014 confirmed independent producer\\n- AVAILABLE: .claude/rules/ directory \u2014 scope boundary context confirmed\\n- AVAILABLE: commit 0e40b24 \u2014 ARL-derived gates pattern confirmed in git history\\n- AVAILABLE: backlog-item-lifecycle.md \u2014 state machine contract confirmed\\n- AVAILABLE: skills/backlog/references/state-machine.md \u2014 in-progress state definition confirmed\\n- AVAILABLE: quick/start.md gate_token parameter \u2014 RESOLVED: proactive-fix gate adds tasks to the plan when no backlog item exists; no backlog_add call is made\\n- AVAILABLE: All RT-ICA conditions satisfied\\n\\n**Resolution of prior MISSING condition:**\\ngate_token for backlog_add: RESOLVED \u2014 user decision 2026-05-22: when proactive-fix gate fires and no backlog item exists, add tasks to the plan directly. No backlog_add is needed.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Producers\\n\\n- `.claude/CLAUDE.md::Autonomous Action Boundary` \u2014 This section is the producer of the directive \\\"Fix errors discovered during current task\\\". Adding a three-step pre-fix gate (skill loading \u2192 mission assessment \u2192 --quick routing) changes the behavioral contract encoded here. The table at line 137\u2013146 must be replaced or augmented with the gated procedure. | Risk: HIGH | Why: This is the root source of the uncoupled directive; any fix to the process must originate here. All agents read CLAUDE.md at session start \u2014 a malformed or incomplete update causes incorrect behavior across all sessions.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/quick/start.md` \u2014 Defines the --quick workflow: item lookup, SAM plan creation (single T1 task, complexity=low), backlog_update to link plan slug. This is the workflow that proactive fixes must route through. Adding a new entry-point from CLAUDE.md's gate will require this document to be accurate and complete as the specification for quick-path execution. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: The workflow is already written and functional; the risk is that the new gate must route callers to invoke it correctly without duplicating or bypassing its steps.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/scripts/parser/parse.mjs` \u2014 Parser that handles `--quick` as `flags.quick = true` (line 112\u2013113) but does not register `--quick` as a registry command in `command-routes.json`. The SKILL.md routing diagram dispatches on `mode value` and checks for `--quick` as a branch. The parser-to-skill handoff is the execution path an agent would traverse when the gate fires. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: If CLAUDE.md gate instructs agents to invoke `/work-backlog-item --quick`, the parser must translate that correctly to trigger the quick workflow in SKILL.md. Current path works; adding documentation of it must be accurate.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Consumers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md::P4_LINK` \u2014 Specifies that `status:in-progress` is set via `backlog_update` at Step 7 of work-backlog-item (line 421). Proactive fixes that bypass this pipeline leave no `in-progress` transition. Any agent reading this lifecycle document would find the state transition unexplained for the quick-path case. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: The lifecycle document becomes partially stale when proactive fixes skip the full pipeline \u2014 no mention of quick-path status transitions or their absence. Section 194 (line) already notes timing constraints on in-progress label.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/references/state-machine.md` \u2014 Defines the backlog state machine including the `in-progress` state (appears at line 51, 98\u2013107, 111\u2013130). A proactive fix with no --quick invocation leaves the item without an `in-progress` label and no SAM plan, creating an invisible execution that contradicts the state machine. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: State machine documents what must happen; proactive fixes as currently implemented violate this contract silently.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/scripts/parser/command-routes.json` \u2014 Registry of named routes (`create`, `groom`, `work`, `close`, `resolve`, `setup-github`, `progress`, `resume`). `--quick` is NOT in this registry (it is handled as a flag, not a positional command). This is a structural gap \u2014 the quick path is handled via flag routing in SKILL.md rather than command routing in the JSON registry. The three-step gate design must account for this distinction. | Risk: LOW | Why: The current architecture is consistent (flag not command); the risk is that any documentation of the gate incorrectly describes --quick as a subcommand.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/scripts/parser/parser-guide.md` \u2014 Documents how the parser works including flag and command detection. If the gate adds new entry conventions (e.g. auto-invoking `--quick` from CLAUDE.md), parser-guide.md must reflect the intended invocation form. | Risk: LOW | Why: Documentation of parser behaviour; would become stale if the invocation form changes.\\n\\n### Documentation\\n\\n- `.claude/CLAUDE.md::Pre-Existing Issue Accountability` \u2014 The \\\"Pre-Existing Issue Accountability\\\" section (lines 262\u2013276) defines the trigger-to-act rule: offer to plan or add to backlog. This is separate from the Autonomous Action Boundary. Together, these two sections define when proactive fixes happen and what follows \u2014 but they do not reference each other or reference the --quick path. When the gate is added, both sections must be coherent. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: An agent reading only one section gets incomplete instructions. The pre-existing issue rule says \\\"fix it or add to backlog\\\" without gating on skill loading or complexity.\\n\\n- `.claude/CLAUDE.md::Request Progression` \u2014 Section at lines 278\u2013290 defines three progression steps (Backlog \u2192 Plan \u2192 Progress). Explicitly carves out \\\"trivial single-step requests\\\" from the requirement to create backlog items. This exemption clause partially overlaps the intent of --quick, creating an ambiguous boundary. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: If a proactive fix qualifies as \\\"trivial single-step,\\\" the agent may conclude no --quick invocation is needed. This loophole must be closed or the exemption criteria must be made consistent with the three-step gate's complexity check.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md::Routing` \u2014 Routing diagram (lines 156\u2013189) shows `--quick` as a first-class route branching off at `mode value?`. This is correct behaviour but it is not referenced from CLAUDE.md's Autonomous Action Boundary. Any agent acting on the directive without loading this skill would not know the --quick path exists. | Risk: HIGH | Why: The coupling gap is structural \u2014 CLAUDE.md and work-backlog-item SKILL.md are independent documents with no cross-reference at the proactive-fix junction. Agents operating in sessions without the dh skill loaded cannot route through --quick even if they know about it.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-lifecycle.md` \u2014 Contains the full lifecycle state diagram and phase descriptions. If quick-path is used for proactive fixes, the lifecycle document should document this as a valid abbreviated path (not just the full grooming \u2192 RT-ICA \u2192 SAM \u2192 in-progress pipeline). | Risk: LOW | Why: Currently the lifecycle documentation only covers the full pipeline. Quick-path is acknowledged implicitly via feasibility-gate.md (offers --quick when over-engineering detected) but not described as a standalone entry-point for proactive fixes.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/feasibility-gate.md::Criterion 4` \u2014 Criterion 4 (line 46) already surfaces the --quick path when a 1-file scope has 4+ tasks: \\\"offer --quick path\\\". This is the only other location in the dh workflow where --quick is recommended as a routing decision. When the CLAUDE.md gate is added, this reference and the new gate must be consistent in how they define complexity eligibility for --quick. | Risk: LOW | Why: Existing documented criterion already uses --quick for scope decisions; new gate must use compatible criteria.\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\nNone identified. No CI workflow files, YAML configs, or environment variables reference the Autonomous Action Boundary directive or the --quick workflow path.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/skills/holistic-linting/references/pre-existing-issues-protocol.md` \u2014 Defines the \\\"fix it now\\\" instruction for blocking pre-existing issues (the flowchart at the top: \\\"Fix it now \u2014 treat as a current issue\\\"). This is a parallel proactive-fix directive that bypasses the --quick path for the same reason as CLAUDE.md. If the new gate applies to all proactive fixes, this protocol must also be updated to gate on skill loading and route through --quick. | Risk: HIGH | Why: This is a second producer of the uncoupled directive pattern. Fixing CLAUDE.md alone while leaving this protocol untouched creates two divergent behavior specifications in the same repository.\\n\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/agents/linting-root-cause-resolver.md` \u2014 Agent that follows the pre-existing issues protocol. The agent's instructions at lines 110\u2013129 say \\\"fix it now\\\" for blocking pre-existing issues. If --quick routing is required for all proactive fixes, this agent must be updated to route blocking pre-existing issue fixes through --quick. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Agent-level instruction conflict. The agent operates autonomously and does not currently invoke --quick for blocking fixes it encounters.\\n\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/skills/holistic-linting-orchestrator/SKILL.md` \u2014 Orchestrator skill that instructs delegation to linting-root-cause-resolver; notes that pre-existing blocking issues get fixed immediately. If the gate requires --quick for those fixes, the orchestrator must be updated to instruct the resolver to route through --quick instead of acting directly. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Orchestrator instructions become stale or contradict the new gate.\\n\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/skills/holistic-linting/SKILL.md` \u2014 Core linting skill with \\\"fix it now\\\" reference via the pre-existing issues protocol. If the gate requirement changes behaviour for blocking issues, this skill's guidance must be updated to reflect the gated behaviour. | Risk: LOW | Why: References the protocol by pointer; update to the protocol file is the primary change needed, but the core skill may need a note on scope.\\n\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/agents/post-linting-architecture-reviewer.md` \u2014 Reviews resolution reports including pre-existing issues recorded section. If pre-existing blocking issues now route through --quick rather than direct fix, the reviewer's expected report format must account for the changed output shape. | Risk: LOW | Why: Report format change is secondary but the reviewer's validation criteria may reference a direct-fix pattern.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n- `.claude/CLAUDE.md` | Role: producer | Connection: Contains the Autonomous Action Boundary table with the \\\"Fix errors discovered during current task\\\" directive \u2014 the root of the gap | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/quick/start.md` | Role: producer | Connection: Defines the --quick workflow that must be the mandatory routing target for proactive fixes | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: Contains the routing diagram that dispatches --quick; not cross-referenced from CLAUDE.md | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/scripts/parser/parse.mjs` | Role: other-reference | Connection: Handles --quick as a flag, not a registry command \u2014 gate design must account for this | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/scripts/parser/command-routes.json` | Role: other-reference | Connection: Does not include --quick; gap between flag-based routing and command-based routing | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/scripts/parser/parser-guide.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: Documents parser behaviour; may become stale if invocation form changes | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: Defines in-progress state transition sequence; quick-path proactive fixes skip this pipeline | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/references/state-machine.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: State machine definition; proactive fixes as-is violate the in-progress timing constraint | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-lifecycle.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: Full lifecycle; does not describe --quick as a standalone entry point | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/feasibility-gate.md` | Role: other-reference | Connection: Only other location that surfaces --quick as a complexity-routing recommendation; new gate must be consistent with Criterion 4 | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `.claude/CLAUDE.md::Pre-Existing Issue Accountability` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: Separate proactive-fix directive that also lacks --quick coupling; must be coherent with new gate | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `.claude/CLAUDE.md::Request Progression` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: \\\"Trivial single-step\\\" exemption overlaps with --quick eligibility criteria; ambiguity creates a loophole | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/skills/holistic-linting/references/pre-existing-issues-protocol.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: Second producer of uncoupled proactive-fix directive; says \\\"Fix it now\\\" for blocking issues without --quick gate | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/agents/linting-root-cause-resolver.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: Follows pre-existing-issues-protocol; fixes blocking issues directly without --quick | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/skills/holistic-linting-orchestrator/SKILL.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: Orchestrates resolver; instructs direct fix for blocking pre-existing issues | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/skills/holistic-linting/SKILL.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: References pre-existing issues protocol by pointer | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/agents/post-linting-architecture-reviewer.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: Reviews resolution reports; report format may change if routing changes | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n\\n- Overall system risk: HIGH\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `.claude/CLAUDE.md::Autonomous Action Boundary` \u2014 root directive is the source of the gap; changing it affects every agent session\\n  - `plugins/holistic-linting/skills/holistic-linting/references/pre-existing-issues-protocol.md` \u2014 second independent producer of the same uncoupled directive pattern; fixing only CLAUDE.md leaves this active\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` \u2014 --quick workflow exists and is documented but has no cross-reference from CLAUDE.md's directive; the coupling gap is structural\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - documentation: the directive in CLAUDE.md and the --quick workflow in work-backlog-item SKILL.md are structurally decoupled \u2014 no cross-reference at the proactive-fix junction\\n  - scope: the same uncoupled-directive pattern exists independently in the holistic-linting plugin, expanding the fix scope beyond CLAUDE.md alone\\n  - behavioral: agents following the current directives will continue to bypass --quick silently after any change to CLAUDE.md if the holistic-linting protocol is not updated\\n  - verification: no test or checklist currently validates that proactive fixes invoke --quick; the gap is undetectable by automated means\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - The holistic-linting plugin (`pre-existing-issues-protocol.md`, `linting-root-cause-resolver.md`, `holistic-linting-orchestrator/SKILL.md`) contains an independent proactive-fix directive that was not in the original item description. This expands the fix scope to cover the linting plugin's agent instructions as well as CLAUDE.md. Planning and fact-check scope should include these three files.\\n  - The `.claude/CLAUDE.md::Request Progression` section's \\\"trivial single-step\\\" exemption clause creates a loophole that must be addressed as part of the same change.\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated\\n- [ ] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated\\n- [ ] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated\\n- [ ] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved\\n- [ ] Hidden transitive dependencies checked\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAlignment assessment: **ALIGNED**\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\n0 concerns identified \u2014 proposed change is consistent with product mission and historical direction. The three-step pre-fix gate aligns with CLAUDE.md's core principle of structured thinking before action and the development-harness's design pattern of ARL-derived gates (not arbitrary checkpoints). The coupling of \\\"fix it now\\\" to `--quick` routing is an architectural strengthening, not a deviation.\\n\\n### Concerns\\n\\n| Category | Proposed Change Excerpt | Mission Source Violated | Citation |\\n|----------|------------------------|-------------------------|----------|\\n| \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 |\\n\\n### Alignment Rationale\\n\\n**1. Structured thinking before action (fully aligned)**\\n\\nCLAUDE.md explicitly states \\\"Structured thinking before action \u2014 form a hypothesis and plan internally before acting\\\" with a flowchart (lines 67\u201376) that gates destructive/ambiguous work. The current Autonomous Action Boundary table permits \\\"Fix errors discovered during current task\\\" without gates. The proposed three-step gate (skill loading \u2192 alignment check \u2192 backend routing) implements the Structured Thinking principle by adding observable decision points before autonomous action.\\n\\nCitation: `.claude/CLAUDE.md::Critical Constraints::Structured thinking before action (lines 67\u201376)` and `Autonomous Action Boundary table (line 145)`\\n\\n**2. Scientific Engineering Agent identity (fully aligned)**\\n\\nCLAUDE.md identifies the agent role as \\\"Scientific Engineering Agent\\\" that values \\\"observable facts over assumptions and reproducibility over speed\\\" (line 46). The current proactive-fix pattern lacks observable decision points \u2014 the decision to bypass the harness process is made silently. The proposed alignment check ensures observable evidence before acting, strengthening the scientific engineering stance.\\n\\nCitation: `.claude/CLAUDE.md::Identity & Role (lines 44\u201346)`\\n\\n**3. Development-harness design principle: ARL-derived gates, not arbitrary (fully aligned)**\\n\\nThe development-harness CLAUDE.md states \\\"Human escalation follows ARL constraint analysis, not arbitrary checkpoints\\\" (line 17). The proposed three-step gate is not an arbitrary checkpoint \u2014 it is a structural consequence of two design commitments: (a) the harness owns process through the SAM 7-stage pipeline, and (b) the Dispatch Pattern requires `dh:task-worker` for all dh workflows (line 211). The `--quick` routing ensures the SAM lifecycle (claim \u2192 execute \u2192 sam_state) is owned by an agent with full dh tool permissions, not bypassed entirely.\\n\\nCitation: `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md::Design Principles (line 17)` and `Dispatch Pattern Extension Rules (lines 209\u2013241)`\\n\\n**4. Artifact management principle (fully aligned)**\\n\\nThe development-harness states \\\"Every stage produces an MCP-registered artifact (stateless handoff)\\\" and \\\"stored via `artifact_register` and retrieved via `artifact_read`, never via direct filesystem paths\\\" (line 16). The current proactive-fix pattern leaves no audit trail and does not create SAM plan artifacts. The proposed `--quick` routing ensures artifacts are registered through the backend MCP, maintaining the stateless handoff principle.\\n\\nCitation: `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md::Design Principles (line 16)`\\n\\n**5. Historical direction: gates and verification (aligned with direction)**\\n\\nRecent commits show a pattern of adding classification gates to prevent intent loss: commit `0e40b24` (\\\"fix(work-backlog-item): add classification gate to create workflow to prevent intent loss for behavioral/process items\\\") demonstrates that the repository values explicit gates at decision boundaries. The proposed pre-fix gate follows this established pattern.\\n\\nCitation: `git log commit 0e40b24`\\n\\n### No divergences\\n\\nThe proposed change does not:\\n- Remove or weaken the \\\"fix it now\\\" autonomy \u2014 it adds observable gates\\n- Require arbitrary human checkpoints \u2014 the gates are structural consequences of existing design principles\\n- Bypass the harness process \u2014 it routes through the defined `--quick` path instead of avoiding it\\n- Change the harness's role or ownership \u2014 it strengthens process tracking while preserving autonomous fix capability\\n\\nProposed implementation is consistent with the system's stated mission to provide \\\"language-agnostic development process harness that orchestrates feature development through a structured 7-stage pipeline\\\" with gates \\\"derived from constraint analysis, not arbitrary checkpoints.\\\"\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **proactive fix directive is decoupled from --quick path: no skill loading gate, no complexity check, no backend state tracking** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 #2371 resolution (2026-05-22)\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-05-22\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n\\n<!-- artifact-gist:7858087e80ed2232266a2e4a24cd8565 -->\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-05-22)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Open CLAUDE.md and locate the \\\"Pre-Existing Issue Accountability\\\" directive \u2014 the rule states \\\"Phrase 'pre-existing issues not related to my changes' is a TRIGGER TO ACT, not a dismissal justification.\\\"\\n2. Observe that no gate, conditional, or guard clause follows the directive that would route the agent through `/dh:work-backlog-item --quick` before acting.\\n3. Open `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` (or equivalent quick-path documentation). Confirm that no reference to the Pre-Existing Issue Accountability directive, the proactive-fix pattern, or CLAUDE.md coupling exists in the `--quick` flow entry point.\\n4. Open `plugins/holistic-linting/skills/holistic-linting-orchestrator/SKILL.md`, `pre-existing-issues-protocol.md`, and `linting-root-cause-resolver.md`. Observe each contains a proactive-fix directive with no coupling to the dh status machine.\\n5. Simulate invoking a proactive fix: act on a visible defect without loading a domain skill, without a mission-objective check, and without the `--quick` flag. Observe that no `in-progress` label, SAM plan stub, or audit trail is created in the backlog.\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n8/10 \u2014 P1.\\n\\nThe impact radius spans 17 systems, including CLAUDE.md (the root behavioral contract), the holistic-linting plugin (three files with an independent instance of the same uncoupled pattern), and the dh status machine that all tracked work flows through. Every proactive fix that bypasses this gate produces an audit gap \u2014 work done with no `in-progress` label, no SAM plan stub, and no backlog item trail. The gap compounds with frequency: the proactive-fix directive fires on every observed defect, meaning each untracked fix is an unreproducible, unreviewed change. The missing-guardrail classification confirms this is structural absence rather than user error. P0 is not warranted because existing work is not broken \u2014 the status machine accepts all work that flows through it correctly; the gap is in routing, not in processing.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- **Blocks**: Every proactive fix that fires bypasses the dh status machine \u2014 no `in-progress` label is set, no SAM plan stub is created, and no audit trail exists. Downstream review, quality gates, and completion verification cannot run against work that was never registered.\\n- **Bottleneck**: The absence of a skill-loading gate means agents acting on proactive-fix directives operate without domain context, treating plugin markdown as static documentation rather than behavioral engineering specifications. This degrades fix quality independent of the tracking gap.\\n- **Scope**: The gap exists in at least four locations \u2014 CLAUDE.md and three holistic-linting files \u2014 meaning it fires across both the main harness behavioral contract and the linting orchestration sub-system.\\n- **Affected parties**: Agents executing proactive fixes (all agents with CLAUDE.md loaded); the dh SAM pipeline (receives no work registration signal); code reviewers (no PR or plan stub to review against); the holistic-linting orchestrator (independent instance of the same gap, fires independently).\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Every proactive fix becomes a tracked, auditable work item \u2014 visible in the backlog with `in-progress` status and a SAM plan stub before any code changes are made.\\n- Domain skills load before agents act, ensuring plugin markdown is read as behavioral specification rather than static documentation.\\n- Mission-objective assessment fires before a fix is committed, enabling evaluation against design principles and scope boundaries.\\n- Complexity classification distinguishes trivial one-liners (handled inline) from multi-file changes (routed to full planning), preventing scope creep from proactive-fix triggers.\\n- The holistic-linting plugin gains the same coupling, closing an independent instance of the same gap without requiring separate future work.\\n- The `--quick` path gains a defined entry point for the proactive-fix class of work, making its use observable and measurable.\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen an agent encounters a visible defect that triggers the proactive-fix directive:\\n\\n1. The agent pauses before acting and checks whether the required domain skill is loaded. If not, the skill loads before any fix proceeds.\\n2. The agent evaluates the fix against the mission objective before acting \u2014 not after.\\n3. The agent invokes the `--quick` path (or equivalent routing) to register the work item in the dh status machine. The backlog item receives an `in-progress` label and a SAM plan stub is created before any code changes are made.\\n4. The fix proceeds with domain context active, status tracking live, and the work item visible to downstream quality gates.\\n5. The same behavior fires from all four locations that contain the proactive-fix directive: CLAUDE.md and the three holistic-linting files.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. Read CLAUDE.md \u2014 the Pre-Existing Issue Accountability section references the `--quick` path (or equivalent dh routing) and the domain skill gate. The coupling is present in the directive text, not only in a separate rule file.\\n2. Read each of the three holistic-linting files (`pre-existing-issues-protocol.md`, `linting-root-cause-resolver.md`, `holistic-linting-orchestrator/SKILL.md`) \u2014 each contains an equivalent coupling to the dh status machine and the skill-loading gate. No file retains an uncoupled proactive-fix directive.\\n3. Run `grep -r \\\"proactive\\\" plugins/holistic-linting/ .claude/CLAUDE.md` \u2014 every match is co-located with a `--quick` routing reference or equivalent dh status machine invocation within the same section.\\n4. Simulate invoking the proactive-fix directive path as described in the updated CLAUDE.md \u2014 a backlog item with `in-progress` label and a SAM plan stub appear in the dh backlog before any code change is staged.\\n5. The `--quick` invocation form used in all updated files matches the parser flag form (`flags.quick = true`) confirmed by the fact-check pass \u2014 not a registry command form.\\n6. A complexity check observable in the updated directive text distinguishes trivial fixes (handled without a full plan) from multi-file changes (routed to planning). The criteria for each category are stated explicitly enough that two agents reading the text would classify the same example the same way.\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Skill | `/dh:work-backlog-item` \u2014 the `--quick` path this item couples to; read SKILL.md to confirm invocation form and gate sequence |\\n| Skill | `/dh:rt-ica` \u2014 the blocking implementation gate; understanding its trigger pattern clarifies where the grooming-phase gate fits |\\n| Skill | `/dh:planner-rt-ica` \u2014 the planning-phase non-blocking variant; models the pattern of adding gates without halting work |\\n| Skill | `/holistic-linting:holistic-linting-orchestrator` \u2014 contains the independent proactive-fix directive to be updated |\\n| Rule file | `.claude/CLAUDE.md` \u2014 source of truth for the Pre-Existing Issue Accountability directive and \\\"Structured thinking before action\\\" flowchart |\\n| Rule file | `.claude/rules/` \u2014 directory containing supporting behavioral rules referenced by CLAUDE.md (scope-boundary context for what should vs. should not go in CLAUDE.md vs. a separate rule file) |\\n| Prior work | commit `0e40b24` \u2014 added classification gates; established the ARL-derived gates pattern this item extends |\\n| Related item | #2371 \u2014 the resolution session that surfaced this gap (source of the observation) |\\n| Related item | #2406 \u2014 gate token fix (related but not a blocker; the `--quick` path gate token is a separate concern) |\\n\\n### Files\\n\\nFiles requiring modification to close the gap (from impact radius):\\n\\n| File | Change type | Reason |\\n|------|-------------|--------|\\n| `.claude/CLAUDE.md` | Modify | Primary location of the proactive-fix directive \u2014 add skill-loading gate, mission-objective check, complexity classification, and `--quick` routing coupling |\\n| `plugins/holistic-linting/skills/holistic-linting-orchestrator/SKILL.md` | Modify | Contains independent proactive-fix directive with the same uncoupled pattern |\\n| `plugins/holistic-linting/references/pre-existing-issues-protocol.md` (or equivalent path) | Modify | Contains proactive-fix directive without dh status machine coupling |\\n| `plugins/holistic-linting/references/linting-root-cause-resolver.md` (or equivalent path) | Modify | Contains proactive-fix directive without dh status machine coupling |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/quick/start.md` (or equivalent `--quick` reference doc) | Modify or create | Document that the `--quick` path is the canonical entry point for the proactive-fix class of work; confirm invocation form is the parser flag `flags.quick = true` |\\n\\nNote: Exact paths for holistic-linting references should be verified against the live filesystem before planning \u2014 the swarm identified the files by content, not by confirmed absolute path.\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 all RT-ICA conditions are AVAILABLE or DERIVABLE; no upstream items must complete first.\\n- Blocks: Any item that assumes proactive-fix directives are correctly routed through the dh status machine \u2014 until this item is resolved, that assumption is false.\\n- Related (not blocking): #2406 (gate token fix) \u2014 addresses the `--quick` path gate token separately; coordinate to avoid conflicting edits to work-backlog-item skill files.\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nMedium \u2014 3\u20135 story points.\\n\\nRationale: The change is editorial and additive \u2014 coupling language inserted into existing directive sections in five files, with no new code, no schema changes, and no new skill creation required. The complexity is in precision: the gate language must be unambiguous enough to satisfy AC #6 (two agents classify the same example the same way), and the `--quick` invocation form must be verified against the parser flag before any file is updated. The holistic-linting files require path verification before editing. The work can proceed in parallel across the five files once the invocation form is confirmed. No external dependencies are unresolved.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/skills/holistic-linting/references/pre-existing-issues-protocol.md` \u2014 Defines the \\\"fix it now\\\" instruction for blocking pre-existing issues (the flowchart at the top: \\\"Fix it now \u2014 treat as a current issue\\\"). This is a parallel proactive-fix directive that bypasses the --quick path for the same reason as CLAUDE.md. If the new gate applies to all proactive fixes, this protocol must also be updated to gate on skill loading and route through --quick. | Risk: HIGH | Why: This is a second producer of the uncoupled directive pattern. Fixing CLAUDE.md alone while leaving this protocol untouched creates two divergent behavior specifications in the same repository.\\n\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/agents/linting-root-cause-resolver.md` \u2014 Agent that follows the pre-existing issues protocol. The agent's instructions at lines 110\u2013129 say \\\"fix it now\\\" for blocking pre-existing issues. If --quick routing is required for all proactive fixes, this agent must be updated to route blocking pre-existing issue fixes through --quick. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Agent-level instruction conflict. The agent operates autonomously and does not currently invoke --quick for blocking fixes it encounters.\\n\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/skills/holistic-linting-orchestrator/SKILL.md` \u2014 Orchestrator skill that instructs delegation to linting-root-cause-resolver; notes that pre-existing blocking issues get fixed immediately. If the gate requires --quick for those fixes, the orchestrator must be updated to instruct the resolver to route through --quick instead of acting directly. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Orchestrator instructions become stale or contradict the new gate.\\n\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/skills/holistic-linting/SKILL.md` \u2014 Core linting skill with \\\"fix it now\\\" reference via the pre-existing issues protocol. If the gate requirement changes behaviour for blocking issues, this skill's guidance must be updated to reflect the gated behaviour. | Risk: LOW | Why: References the protocol by pointer; update to the protocol file is the primary change needed, but the core skill may need a note on scope.\\n\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/agents/post-linting-architecture-reviewer.md` \u2014 Reviews resolution reports including pre-existing issues recorded section. If pre-existing blocking issues now route through --quick rather than direct fix, the reviewer's expected report format must account for the changed output shape. | Risk: LOW | Why: Report format change is secondary but the reviewer's validation criteria may reference a direct-fix pattern.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions (instruct AI to use current interface)\\n\\n- `.claude/CLAUDE.md` \u2014 primary AI behavioral instruction file affected by this change\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/skills/holistic-linting-orchestrator/SKILL.md` \u2014 secondary AI instruction file with parallel uncoupled directive\\n\\n### Alignment Rationale\\n\\n**1. Structured thinking before action (fully aligned)**\\n\\nCLAUDE.md explicitly states \\\"Structured thinking before action \u2014 form a hypothesis and plan internally before acting\\\" with a flowchart (lines 67\u201376) that gates destructive/ambiguous work. The current Autonomous Action Boundary table permits \\\"Fix errors discovered during current task\\\" without gates. The proposed three-step gate (skill loading \u2192 alignment check \u2192 backend routing) implements the Structured Thinking principle by adding observable decision points before autonomous action.\\n\\nCitation: `.claude/CLAUDE.md::Critical Constraints::Structured thinking before action (lines 67\u201376)` and `Autonomous Action Boundary table (line 145)`\\n\\n**2. Scientific Engineering Agent identity (fully aligned)**\\n\\nCLAUDE.md identifies the agent role as \\\"Scientific Engineering Agent\\\" that values \\\"observable facts over assumptions and reproducibility over speed\\\" (line 46). The current proactive-fix pattern lacks observable decision points \u2014 the decision to bypass the harness process is made silently. The proposed alignment check ensures observable evidence before acting, strengthening the scientific engineering stance.\\n\\nCitation: `.claude/CLAUDE.md::Identity & Role (lines 44\u201346)`\\n\\n**3. Development-harness design principle: ARL-derived gates, not arbitrary (fully aligned)**\\n\\nThe development-harness CLAUDE.md states \\\"Human escalation follows ARL constraint analysis, not arbitrary checkpoints\\\" (line 17). The proposed three-step gate is not an arbitrary checkpoint \u2014 it is a structural consequence of two design commitments: (a) the harness owns process through the SAM 7-stage pipeline, and (b) the Dispatch Pattern requires `dh:task-worker` for all dh workflows (line 211). The `--quick` routing ensures the SAM lifecycle (claim \u2192 execute \u2192 sam_state) is owned by an agent with full dh tool permissions, not bypassed entirely.\\n\\nCitation: `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md::Design Principles (line 17)` and `Dispatch Pattern Extension Rules (lines 209\u2013241)`\\n\\n**4. Artifact management principle (fully aligned)**\\n\\nThe development-harness states \\\"Every stage produces an MCP-registered artifact (stateless handoff)\\\" and \\\"stored via `artifact_register` and retrieved via `artifact_read`, never via direct filesystem paths\\\" (line 16). The current proactive-fix pattern leaves no audit trail and does not create SAM plan artifacts. The proposed `--quick` routing ensures artifacts are registered through the backend MCP, maintaining the stateless handoff principle.\\n\\nCitation: `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md::Design Principles (line 16)`\\n\\n**5. Historical direction: gates and verification (aligned with direction)**\\n\\nRecent commits show a pattern of adding classification gates to prevent intent loss: commit `0e40b24` (\\\"fix(work-backlog-item): add classification gate to create workflow to prevent intent loss for behavioral/process items\\\") demonstrates that the repository values explicit gates at decision boundaries. The proposed pre-fix gate follows this established pattern.\\n\\nCitation: `git log commit 0e40b24`\\n\\n### Code - Consumers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md::P4_LINK` \u2014 Specifies that `status:in-progress` is set via `backlog_update` at Step 7 of work-backlog-item (line 421). Proactive fixes that bypass this pipeline leave no `in-progress` transition. Any agent reading this lifecycle document would find the state transition unexplained for the quick-path case. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: The lifecycle document becomes partially stale when proactive fixes skip the full pipeline \u2014 no mention of quick-path status transitions or their absence.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/references/state-machine.md` \u2014 Defines the backlog state machine including the `in-progress` state (appears at line 51, 98\u2013107, 111\u2013130). A proactive fix with no --quick invocation leaves the item without an `in-progress` label and no SAM plan, creating an invisible execution that contradicts the state machine. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: State machine documents what must happen; proactive fixes as currently implemented violate this contract silently.\\n\\n### Code - Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/scripts/parser/command-routes.json` \u2014 Registry of named routes (`create`, `groom`, `work`, `close`, `resolve`, `setup-github`, `progress`, `resume`). `--quick` is NOT in this registry (it is handled as a flag, not a positional command). This is a structural gap \u2014 the quick path is handled via flag routing in SKILL.md rather than command routing in the JSON registry. The three-step gate design must account for this distinction. | Risk: LOW | Why: The current architecture is consistent (flag not command); the risk is that any documentation of the gate incorrectly describes --quick as a subcommand.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/scripts/parser/parser-guide.md` \u2014 Documents how the parser works including flag and command detection. If the gate adds new entry conventions (e.g. auto-invoking `--quick` from CLAUDE.md), parser-guide.md must reflect the intended invocation form. | Risk: LOW | Why: Documentation of parser behaviour; would become stale if the invocation form changes.\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n\\n- `.claude/CLAUDE.md::Autonomous Action Boundary` \u2014 This section is the producer of the directive \\\"Fix errors discovered during current task\\\". Adding a three-step pre-fix gate (skill loading \u2192 mission assessment \u2192 --quick routing) changes the behavioral contract encoded here. The table at line 137\u2013146 must be replaced or augmented with the gated procedure. | Risk: HIGH | Why: This is the root source of the uncoupled directive; any fix to the process must originate here. All agents read CLAUDE.md at session start \u2014 a malformed or incomplete update causes incorrect behavior across all sessions.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/quick/start.md` \u2014 Defines the --quick workflow: item lookup, SAM plan creation (single T1 task, complexity=low), backlog_update to link plan slug. This is the workflow that proactive fixes must route through. Adding a new entry-point from CLAUDE.md's gate will require this document to be accurate and complete as the specification for quick-path execution. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: The workflow is already written and functional; the risk is that the new gate must route callers to invoke it correctly without duplicating or bypassing its steps.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/scripts/parser/parse.mjs` \u2014 Parser that handles `--quick` as `flags.quick = true` (line 112\u2013113) but does not register `--quick` as a registry command in `command-routes.json`. The SKILL.md routing diagram dispatches on `mode value` and checks for `--quick` as a branch. The parser-to-skill handoff is the execution path an agent would traverse when the gate fires. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: If CLAUDE.md gate instructs agents to invoke `/work-backlog-item --quick`, the parser must translate that correctly to trigger the quick workflow in SKILL.md. Current path works; adding documentation of it must be accurate.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Consumers\\n\\n- None identified.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` \u2014 --quick routing diagram exists but no cross-reference from CLAUDE.md directive\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/quick/start.md` \u2014 --quick workflow definition; correct invocation is a parser flag (flags.quick = true), not a registry command\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Producers\\n\\n- `.claude/CLAUDE.md::Autonomous Action Boundary` \u2014 root directive table; new pre-fix gate section required adjacent to lines 137\u2013146\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/skills/holistic-linting/references/pre-existing-issues-protocol.md` \u2014 independent proactive-fix directive with same uncoupled pattern\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/skills/holistic-linting/references/linting-root-cause-resolver.md` \u2014 fires direct fixes without --quick routing\\n- `plugins/holistic-linting/skills/holistic-linting-orchestrator/SKILL.md` \u2014 orchestrator instructs direct fixes without --quick gating\\n\\n### Concerns\\n\\n| Category | Proposed Change Excerpt | Mission Source Violated | Citation |\\n|----------|------------------------|-------------------------|----------|\\n| \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 | \u2014 |\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\nNone identified. No CI workflow files, YAML configs, or environment variables reference the Autonomous Action Boundary directive or the --quick workflow path.\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\nAlignment assessment: **ALIGNED**\\n\\n### Documentation\\n\\n- `.claude/CLAUDE.md::Pre-Existing Issue Accountability` \u2014 The \\\"Pre-Existing Issue Accountability\\\" section (lines 262\u2013276) defines the trigger-to-act rule: offer to plan or add to backlog. This is separate from the Autonomous Action Boundary. Together, these two sections define when proactive fixes happen and what follows \u2014 but they do not reference each other or reference the --quick path. When the gate is added, both sections must be coherent. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: An agent reading only one section gets incomplete instructions. The pre-existing issue rule says \\\"fix it or add to backlog\\\" without gating on skill loading or complexity.\\n\\n- `.claude/CLAUDE.md::Request Progression` \u2014 Section at lines 278\u2013290 defines three progression steps (Backlog \u2192 Plan \u2192 Progress). Explicitly carves out \\\"trivial single-step requests\\\" from the requirement to create backlog items. This exemption clause partially overlaps the intent of --quick, creating an ambiguous boundary. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: If a proactive fix qualifies as \\\"trivial single-step,\\\" the agent may conclude no --quick invocation is needed. This loophole must be closed or the exemption criteria must be made consistent with the three-step gate's complexity check.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md::Routing` \u2014 Routing diagram (lines 156\u2013189) shows `--quick` as a first-class route branching off at `mode value?`. This is correct behaviour but it is not referenced from CLAUDE.md's Autonomous Action Boundary. Any agent acting on the directive without loading this skill would not know the --quick path exists. | Risk: HIGH | Why: The coupling gap is structural \u2014 CLAUDE.md and work-backlog-item SKILL.md are independent documents with no cross-reference at the proactive-fix junction. Agents operating in sessions without the dh skill loaded cannot route through --quick even if they know about it.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-lifecycle.md` \u2014 Contains the full lifecycle state diagram and phase descriptions. If quick-path is used for proactive fixes, the lifecycle document should document this as a valid abbreviated path (not just the full grooming \u2192 RT-ICA \u2192 SAM \u2192 in-progress pipeline). | Risk: LOW | Why: Currently the lifecycle documentation only covers the full pipeline. Quick-path is acknowledged implicitly via feasibility-gate.md (offers --quick when over-engineering detected) but not described as a standalone entry-point for proactive fixes.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/feasibility-gate.md::Criterion 4` \u2014 Criterion 4 (line 46) already surfaces the --quick path when a 1-file scope has 4+ tasks: \\\"offer --quick path\\\". This is the only other location in the dh workflow where --quick is recommended as a routing decision. When the CLAUDE.md gate is added, this reference and the new gate must be consistent in how they define complexity eligibility for --quick. | Risk: LOW | Why: Existing documented criterion already uses --quick for scope decisions; new gate must use compatible criteria.\\n\\n### Documentation (will become stale)\\n\\n- `.claude/CLAUDE.md` \u2014 Autonomous Action Boundary section will require new pre-fix gate content\\n- `.claude/rules/` \u2014 any rule files referencing proactive fix behavior\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated\\n- [ ] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated\\n- [ ] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated\\n- [ ] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved\\n- [ ] Hidden transitive dependencies checked\\n\\n### Impact Radius\\n\\n\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Type**: missing-guardrail\\n\\n**Rationale**: The CLAUDE.md directive to \\\"fix visible problems immediately\\\" exists as an independent path with no integration to the `--quick` workflow. The system allows proactive fixes to bypass three validation gates: (1) domain skill loading before acting, (2) complexity assessment to verify the fix qualifies for quick path vs. requiring planning, and (3) automatic routing through `--quick` to ensure proper backend state tracking (in-progress label, SAM plan creation, audit trail). Without these gates, work proceeds off the rails with no skill context verification, no complexity check, and no plan artifact. The observed incident (#2371) demonstrates the consequence: work was correctly scoped but workflow state was not tracked.\\n\\n**Analysis Method**: N/A (missing-guardrail requires no root-cause analysis)\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: Proactive fixes bypass workflow validation and create orphaned work with no audit trail, no skill context, no plan artifact, no in-progress tracking \u2192 Proactive fixes are gated by skill loading verification, complexity check, and automatic `--quick` routing, with full backend state tracking and audit trail\\n\\n### No divergences\\n\\nThe proposed change does not:\\n- Remove or weaken the \\\"fix it now\\\" autonomy \u2014 it adds observable gates\\n- Require arbitrary human checkpoints \u2014 the gates are structural consequences of existing design principles\\n- Bypass the harness process \u2014 it routes through the defined `--quick` path instead of avoiding it\\n- Change the harness's role or ownership \u2014 it strengthens process tracking while preserving autonomous fix capability\\n\\nProposed implementation is consistent with the system's stated mission to provide \\\"language-agnostic development process harness that orchestrates feature development through a structured 7-stage pipeline\\\" with gates \\\"derived from constraint analysis, not arbitrary checkpoints.\\\"\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n\\n- Overall system risk: HIGH\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `.claude/CLAUDE.md::Autonomous Action Boundary` \u2014 root directive is the source of the gap; changing it affects every agent session\\n  - `plugins/holistic-linting/skills/holistic-linting/references/pre-existing-issues-protocol.md` \u2014 second independent producer of the same uncoupled directive pattern; fixing only CLAUDE.md leaves this active\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` \u2014 --quick workflow exists and is documented but has no cross-reference from CLAUDE.md's directive; the coupling gap is structural\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - documentation: the directive in CLAUDE.md and the --quick workflow in work-backlog-item SKILL.md are structurally decoupled \u2014 no cross-reference at the proactive-fix junction\\n  - scope: the same uncoupled-directive pattern exists independently in the holistic-linting plugin, expanding the fix scope beyond CLAUDE.md alone\\n  - behavioral: agents following the current directives will continue to bypass --quick silently after any change to CLAUDE.md if the holistic-linting protocol is not updated\\n  - verification: no test or checklist currently validates that proactive fixes invoke --quick; the gap is undetectable by automated means\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - The holistic-linting plugin (`pre-existing-issues-protocol.md`, `linting-root-cause-resolver.md`, `holistic-linting-orchestrator/SKILL.md`) contains an independent proactive-fix directive that was not in the original item description. This expands the fix scope to cover the linting plugin's agent instructions as well as CLAUDE.md. Planning and fact-check scope should include these three files.\\n  - The `.claude/CLAUDE.md::Request Progression` section's \\\"trivial single-step\\\" exemption clause creates a loophole that must be addressed as part of the same change.\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\n0 concerns identified \u2014 proposed change is consistent with product mission and historical direction. The three-step pre-fix gate aligns with CLAUDE.md's core principle of structured thinking before action and the development-harness's design pattern of ARL-derived gates (not arbitrary checkpoints). The coupling of \\\"fix it now\\\" to `--quick` routing is an architectural strengthening, not a deviation.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n- `.claude/CLAUDE.md` | Role: producer | Connection: Contains the Autonomous Action Boundary table with the \\\"Fix errors discovered during current task\\\" directive \u2014 the root of the gap | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/quick/start.md` | Role: producer | Connection: Defines the --quick workflow that must be the mandatory routing target for proactive fixes | Action: VE